1. What is LDOM?

LDOM (Logical Domain) is a technology that allows you to divide one physical SPARC server into multiple virtual servers.

Each LDOM works like an independent server.

Each LDOM has:

Its own CPU
Its own RAM
Its own Storage
Its own Network
Its own Solaris Operating System

2. Real-Life Example

Imagine you own a 4-floor building.

Instead of giving the whole building to one company,

you rent each floor to different companies.

Building

├── Floor 1 → Company A
├── Floor 2 → Company B
├── Floor 3 → Company C
└── Floor 4 → Company D

Each company works independently.

If Company B has a problem,

Company A, C, and D continue working.

An LDOM works exactly like this.

3.Without LDOM

Suppose you have one SPARC server.

Physical SPARC Server

↓

Solaris

↓

One Banking Application

Only one operating system is running.

Many hardware resources remain unused.

4. With LDOM

Now divide the same server into four LDOMs.

Physical SPARC Server

│
├── LDOM1 → Solaris → HR Application
├── LDOM2 → Solaris → Banking Application
├── LDOM3 → Solaris → Database
└── LDOM4 → Solaris → Testing

Now one physical server behaves like four separate servers.

5. Example

A company buys a powerful SPARC server.

Specifications:

CPU = 32 Cores
RAM = 128 GB

Instead of using all resources for one OS,

the administrator creates:

LDOM1
CPU = 8 Cores
RAM = 32 GB

LDOM2
CPU = 8 Cores
RAM = 32 GB

LDOM3
CPU = 8 Cores
RAM = 32 GB

LDOM4
CPU = 8 Cores
RAM = 32 GB

Each department gets its own virtual server.

6. Why do Companies Use LDOM?
a) Better Hardware Utilization

Instead of buying four physical servers,

buy one powerful server and create four LDOMs.

This saves money.

b) Isolation

If one LDOM crashes,
LDOM1 ❌

LDOM2 ✅

LDOM3 ✅

LDOM4 ✅

The other LDOMs continue working.

c) Resource Allocation

Each LDOM gets its own resources.

Example:

HR

CPU = 4 Cores

RAM = 16 GB

---------------------

Database

CPU = 16 Cores

RAM = 64 GB

Resources are assigned based on application needs.

7. LDOM vs VMware

Both create virtual machines, but they are used in different environments.

| VMware                                        | LDOM                                     |
| --------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Works on Intel/AMD servers                    | Works only on Oracle SPARC servers       |
| Creates Virtual Machines (VMs)                | Creates Logical Domains (LDOMs)          |
| Managed using VMware ESXi/vCenter             | Managed using Oracle VM Server for SPARC |
| Mostly used in Linux and Windows environments | Mostly used in Solaris environments      |
